/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A GDI fuel system has two stages.
   - The injector sits in the head with its tip in the chamber, either at the side under the inlet ports or in the centre next to the spark plug.
   - In homogeneous mode the fuel is injected early, during the intake stroke.
   - In stratified mode the fuel is injected late in the compression stroke.
   - Because lean exhaust cannot be cleaned of NOx by a three-way catalyst, stratified engines need a lean NOx trap, and many production GDI engines run homogeneous at all loads and keep direct injection for its cooling and knock benefits.
   - Petrol absorbs its latent heat as it evaporates.
   - ΔT is the fall in charge temperature (K) if all the evaporation heat comes from the air; hfg is the latent heat of petrol (kJ/kg); cp is the specific heat of air, 1.005 kJ/kg K.
*/
