/* book Worked example: Q: A GDI engine injects during the intake stroke at A/F = 14.7. Take the latent heat of petrol as 305 kJ/kg and cp of air as 1.005 kJ/kg K. Estimate the fall in charge temperature if all the evaporation heat comes from the air, and the gain in trapped air mass if the air would otherwise enter at 320 K and the pressure stays the same.
   1. Fuel per kg of air = 1 ÷ 14.7 = 0.0680 kg.
   2. Heat taken from 1 kg of air = 0.0680 × 305 = 20.75 kJ.
   3. ΔT = 20.75 ÷ 1.005 = 20.6 K.
   4. Charge temperature = 320 − 20.6 = 299.4 K.
   5. At the same pressure, density ∝ 1/T, so the air mass rises by 320 ÷ 299.4 = 1.069, about 6.9 %.
   Answer: The charge cools by about 21 K and traps about 7 % more air. It is an upper limit: some of the heat still comes from the walls and some fuel evaporates after the inlet valve closes. */
